{"chunk_id": "3406233c5e79", "text": "After accidentally killing the sage Kindama (disguised as a deer) and receiving the curse that he will die if he approaches his wives sexually, King Pandu falls into deep remorse. He reflects on the destructive power of lust, recalling how his own father\u2019s fate was shaped by passion, and concludes that worldly desires\u2014especially the urge for children\u2014are obstacles to spiritual liberation. Determined to seek salvation, he resolves to renounce royal life and adopt severe asceticism in the forest. Kunti and Madri refuse to abandon him and vow to share his austerities. Accepting their devotion, Pandu formally renounces wealth, gives away his ornaments to Brahmanas, and dismisses his attendants. When news reaches Hastinapura, Dhritarashtra grieves deeply for his brother. This moment marks Pandu\u2019s transformation from king to forest ascetic, shaping the conditions under which the future Pandavas will be born.\nAfter killing the cursed deer, Pandu renounces worldly life, vows severe asceticism with Kunti and Madri, gives away his wealth, and withdraws to the forest.\nThemes: renunciation, ascetic vows, curse and guilt, duty vs desire, royal withdrawal", "vector": [0.017583733424544334, -0.011733918450772762, -0.000598783022724092, 0.005489773117005825, -0.012207115069031715, 0.028254671022295952, -0.01549892220646143, -0.019421657547354698, -0.02061493694782257, -0.022301988676190376, 0.011027551256120205, 0.01603384129703045, 0.016417885199189186, 0.013757008127868176, 0.0026917376089841127, 0.011267579160630703, 0.05088584125041962, -0.019984008744359016, 0.03376844897866249, -0.013283810578286648, -0.021945375949144363, -0.014621106907725334, -0.003257516771554947, -0.0010321184527128935, -0.013544411398470402, 0.020793244242668152, 0.01648646406829357, -0.006916222628206015, 0.007461427710950375, -0.011020693928003311, -0.008517549373209476, -0.0023917031940072775, 0.021396741271018982, -0.019984008744359016, -0.